@0    = ~*Hello, <RACE>. Are you here to listen my stories?*~
@1    = ~Stories? What stories? I mean... who are you?~
@2    = ~No, sorry. I think I was looking for someone else. Goodbye.~
@3    = ~*You have returned! Do you wish to hear one of my stories?*~
@4    = ~Perhaps I do. Tell me a story, wisp.~
@5    = ~*What you should ask is what I *was*. Once, I was a storyteller. I bound my mind and soul to the stories I loved so that I could tell them and ensure they will never be forgotten. Perhaps you would like to listen?*~
@6    = ~Yes. Let's see what you've got.~
@7    = ~Maybe later. I need to go now.~
@8    = ~*I know many stories, <RACE>, more than just one. I could tell you of the librarian who lost his hope... or the great monster... or perhaps the story of the innkeeper's key?*~
@9    = ~Tell me about the librarian.~
@10   = ~Tell me about the monster.~
@11   = ~Tell me about the innkeeper.~
@12   = ~Tell me the story of how you died.~
@13   = ~I don't think I'm in the mood for stories .Goodbye.~
@14   = ~*As you wish.*~
@15   = ~*Once upon a time, there was a librarian who lost his library. It burned down one day when he fell asleep in his chambers, and many citizens blamed him for the loss. They believed he should have been there to prevent it. In despair, he took to drinking, as the man lost not only his library, but also his hope of a good life. Many times he considered ending his misery, yet his fears and doubts prevented him from erasing the last tale he was responsible for.*~
@16   = ~*Inevitably, he lost all of his fortune as well as anything he owned. All he had left were the rags on his shoulders to keep him warm. He doubted anything would change for him, until he met a man... a gentleman who gathered the homeless and lost, giving them food and shelter. The man built a home for many others just like the librarian, and although it was far from luxury, it was comfort nonetheless. The man offered him a place at his side, so that he may find something other than sadness. Seeing nothing to lose, the librarian agreed.*~
@17   = ~*The librarian aided his savior faithfully, serving as his right hand, helping him manage the house and all who lived there. He spoke to other men, learned their tales of how they lost their homes, their feelings, those that they loved and hated. The librarian lived there for many years, listening to the tales of many men.*~
@18   = ~*One day, he learned that he had a chance to rebuild his life within a new library. A different library, but one with great potential, and many new books to protect. It was a chance to redeem himself for his past failures.*~
@19   = ~That was an interesting story. May I hear another?~
@20   = ~Uh, okay. That's enough stories for me. I should go.~
@21   = ~Thank you. Maybe I'll hear another tale when I have time to spare.~
@22   = ~*Ah, an excellent choice! Perhaps you should take a seat, for this tale is quite long. Now then, allow me to begin.*~
@23   = ~*Once upon a time, there lived a fierce beast with a huge tail, as well as claws and fangs sharp enough to rend the flesh off anyone foolish enough to fight it. Many humans from settlements close to the beast's forest lived in fear of the deadly creature. The people called it the 'Love-Ender', due to those that willingly entered the forest to attempt to slay the beast to win the hearts of men and women they loved.*~
@24   = ~*Many failed and lost their lives, save for one. The son of a blacksmith from a humble town north of the forest, seeking the beast's head for a trophy to show his beloved one, a woman who held his heart, and prove his manhood. With his father's finest blade in hand, he went to the forest to find the monster.*~
@25   = ~*The lad spent ten days and nights before he slaughtered the beast and brought its head back to his home. He won the heart of his beloved and the respect of the townsfolk. Every man and woman hailed the slayer and sung his praises. Taverns sang of his strength of arm, keenness of eye and sharpness of mind. But while everyone celebrated the monster's death, a young, adventurous child chose to explore the forest to find the monster's body, and he found it - dead on the grass, surrounded by its children.*~
@26   = ~*The child realized then that the monster had always acted in defense of its children, and men gave it the reputation of an evil predator. It was given the name of 'monster' only because of those who wished to prove themselves by slaying it.*~
@27   = ~*That child was the first to tell the story you have just heard.*~
@28   = ~That was a sad story. May I hear another?~
@29   = ~*The innkeeper it is, then.*~
@30   = ~*This story began not so long ago, in a town that's a tenday's walk from where we are now. There was an inn so large that even the innkeeper himself knew not how many rooms were within. The building appeared small from the outside, yet whenever a traveller walked through the door, they couldn't believe their eyes at what they saw. Some said the place was not from this world, and its keeper was a magical being that took the shape of a man.*~
@31   = ~*He was always fond of those of this world, even though some would disappoint him once in a while. He could not bring himself to dislike other men, and he would laugh, cry, smile and dance with us - even though he never enjoyed dancing. But nothing could last forever, and one day he finally had to leave. Legends say, however, that somewhere in this world is a key that he left behind... a key to his magical inn, just waiting for the right person to find it. Though no one has yet discovered it, some say he still believes it can be found.*~
@32   = ~*Perhaps one day you or someone you know will find that key, and with its finding, change that finder's life forever.*~
@33   = ~*...*~
@34   = ~*That is one tale I never expected to tell. Very well... I think I can tell you of how I became one with the Visionhive.*~
@35   = ~*You know the story of the boy who discovered the body of the monster, who only acted as it did in defense of its children. It was the words of men that branded it a monster. Even now, I only call it such because it is what everyone knew it as... but when I found its little ones, so small and helpless... I decided to become a storyteller to ensure that others knew the truth. Everyone should understand that the creature was no monster.*~
@36   = ~*I don't believe anyone in my story is evil. I don't blame those who follow their ambitions, nor do I blame the creature for defending its hatchlings.*~
@37   = ~*I tell my stories so that others might think and feel. I want others to experience these events even if they weren't there. And my tales affected the life of even one person... why should I not be satisfied?*~
@38   = ~*Eventually, I grew old, and died just outside of this cave. I suppose my spirit became entwined with the stories I told and stayed here. And I think I'm glad for it.*~
@39   = ~I see. May you tell me another story?~
@40   = ~I doubt I'll ever understand you or your motivations. I think I'm done with your stories.~
@41   = ~Thank you for sharing your story. See you next time.~
@42   = ~*Goodbye. May you write the next chapter of your own story.*~
